That detail should not be lost. The framework is not a demand for maximal output. It is a permission structure: $\text{pick one admissible slot first}
\;\Rightarrow\;
\text{stabilize the answer}
\;\Rightarrow\;
\text{expand only if needed}.$ He also says, when the student admits she is not ready, that no one is ever ready. This too is a precise motivational move. The lecture does not promise that pressure disappears. It promises that pressure can be met with a structure.

